**Current support and accepted design.** Template-head directives are implemented. General source and module-root directives are accepted design but are not yet available in the Alpha compiler. The queued general-directives plan introduces `$config` and `$entry`. `config.moth` retains its named `project` and builder-specific records. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template, module-root directives modify their module and other source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### Three attachment locations

The directive's compiler-defined contract determines its kind. **Position never changes a directive's kind.** In particular, a declaration modifier placed first in a root file still modifies the following declaration.

[table:
    [th:
        [: Location]
        [: Target]
        [: Example]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Inside a template head]
        [: That template]
        [: `[$slot title]` or `[$md: text]`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Beginning of a normal module root file]
        [: The containing implicit module root, for a designated root directive only]
        [: `$entry`]
    ]

    [td:
        [: Before a source declaration or statement]
        [: Exactly the next eligible construct]
        [: `$config` followed by `api_url #String`]
    ]
]

A module-root directive must be the first meaningful item in its **root file**, on its own line. Leading whitespace and comments are permitted. It cannot appear in a helper, support facade, configuration file, function, export block or template. Only `$entry` has this role in the accepted design. It marks eligibility for builder entry selection, does not activate the implicit `start` and introduces no binding or scope.

A source modifier attaches to one immediately following eligible construct in the same scope. Its own line is followed by the target's next line, or by contiguous comment-only lines and then the target. **Blank lines between a source modifier and its target are invalid.** Multiple blank lines are not a special case: even one empty line breaks attachment. Comments alone do not break attachment, but a blank line among them does. A modifier cannot cross another source item, an ending scope or a module boundary. Compatible consecutive modifiers may stack without blank lines. A dangling, duplicate or incompatible modifier is an error. The root and template rules do not impose this next-line spacing restriction on template body content or the text following `$entry`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Accepted source modifier syntax:
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"

    -- Accepted module-root syntax (in a normal @ root):
    $entry
]

The examples above describe accepted syntax, not current Alpha compiler support.

### Operands and ownership

The compiler defines directive names, valid target kinds, phases and operand contracts. A directive has at most one optional compile-time operand:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    $name
    $name value
    $name key = value
]

A named operand is one key/value pair, not a parameter list. It has no authored type annotation. Values reuse existing compile-time expression rules where the directive permits a value. A directive may require a specific literal shape, phase or value type. Mutable, runtime or side-effectful operands are invalid. Call-style `$name(...)` and arbitrary `$name: ... ;` source blocks are outside this contract. Ordinary Moth calls such as `f()` are unaffected.

Template heads keep their own delimiters and static slot labels. `$slot title`, `$slot 1`, `$slot title = "Fallback"`, `$in title` and `$fresh` are examples. Current `$before = value` and `$after = value` wrappers migrate to `$before value` and `$after value` under the general plan. `$code("moth")` migrates to a single operand. These are syntax migrations, not new template behaviours. See @../templates/#template-directives (Template directives).

Directives are compiler-owned modifiers, not attributes available to arbitrary source code, macros, user-defined annotations or new independent control-flow constructs. Builders may supply configuration and destination schemas, but cannot register new directive names or semantic callbacks once the general plan lands. Directive names do not reserve matching ordinary source identifiers.

### Examples and contexts

- `$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` constant as an input contract. Its type and fallback are written on the declaration itself. It is valid in selected bootstrap configuration and eligible selected source modules. `#Config of T` remains the operational spelling until this migration.
- `$entry` is a bare, argument-free normal-module-root directive. There is no `$entry()` form and `$page` is not an alias.
- `$test`, `$export`, `$layout` and computation constraints are separate planned designs. Their exact targets and semantics are not implemented by this reference.
- `$async` and `$checked` remain undecided proposals. Their earlier block spellings do not establish a general-purpose directive block grammar.
- Project and active-builder settings remain ordinary named compile-time records inside `config.moth`. There are no accepted `$project`, `$builder` or `$html_builder` directives.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Operational named configuration records:
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )

    html #= ()
]

See @../project-structure/#project-config (Project configuration), @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build configuration values) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).

### Diagnostics and recovery

The compiler recognises a known directive name **before** validating context, so a known directive outside its allowed location receives a placement diagnostic rather than an unknown-directive or generic invalid-character diagnostic. Distinguish unknown names, known but unavailable/deferred names, invalid target kinds, invalid operand shapes, incompatible combinations and root-only placement errors.

Diagnostics should identify the directive's authored span, the attachment it attempted and the nearest useful fix:

- `$entry` after a declaration: move `$entry` to the beginning of the normal root file. A duplicated `$entry` identifies the original marker as well.
- `$entry` in a helper, `+` facade, `config.moth` or nested scope: the directive belongs only at the start of an eligible normal root.
- `$config` separated from its declaration by an empty line or a scope boundary: place the target directly after the modifier. A wrong target, such as a function or local value, identifies the allowed declaration kind.
- `$md` in ordinary source or `$config` inside a template head: use the valid directive context. A `$slot` in a malformed head receives the existing template-head structural diagnostics.
- An unknown directive receives a name diagnostic, while a recognised but not implemented directive receives the established deferred-feature diagnostic. Neither is a successful no-op.

Do not diagnose a valid first-file `$config` as root-scoped. Do not infer directive kind from capitalisation or add a second spelling for root directives. CRLF and LF line endings must give the same results. A malformed directive must not consume unrelated following source during recovery.

